m2c/tests/tools/test_tools_shell.py -- Patch C07: the shell tool v0
(``m2c.tools.shell``).

What is covered (the "Done when" line asks for timeout, kill, env stripping and
truncation; the rest is what the work package lists):

* a normal command and its exit code, a failing command, death by a signal;
* the timeout, and that the WHOLE process group is killed (a child and a
  grandchild are started and both must be gone), including a command that
  ignores SIGTERM and a command that closed its own output;
* cancel through a threading.Event (promptly, and a cancel that is already set
  runs nothing);
* the sanitized environment (fake secrets with many names, the cloud prefixes,
  the extra names from the context, PATH and HOME kept, rc files never sourced);
* output truncation: head and tail, the marker and its exact byte count, a cut
  in the middle of a multi-byte character, and bounded memory for very large
  output (measured with tracemalloc, no gigabytes needed);
* streaming: coalescing, the order of stdout and stderr, escape sequences split
  across reads, control characters, invalid UTF-8;
* stdin is closed, the working directory is pinned, a missing working
  directory is an error and nothing is started;
* authorization fails closed; C06's ``decide`` can still say deny;
* ``timeout_s`` bounds;
* the sandbox warning is given once per session;
* logs never contain the command or its output.

Nothing here looks like a real secret: fake values are built at run time.

Run: c

m2c/tests/tools/test_tools_shell_network.py -- Patch C11b: how the shell tool uses the network proxy (``ShellConfig.network``).

No bubblewrap is needed.  As in test_tools_shell_sandbox.py, ``m2c.sandbox.get_status`` and ``wrap_command`` are fakes
(the wrapper records what it was given and runs the command through a plain ``sh``), but the proxy is REAL: it is started
by the shell tool exactly as in production, on its own thread.  So these tests prove the wiring:

* ``network="off"`` is exactly the C11a behavior: no proxy, no mount, nothing new passed to the wrapper;
* ``network="prompt"`` (the default) starts the proxy lazily, once per session, and gives the wrapper a read-only mount of
  its private directory plus the python that runs the bridge;
* the proxy variables are injected AFTER sanitizing: the host's own proxy settings (any letter case) are replaced,
  ``NO_PROXY`` is empty, and a user's ``extra_strip_names`` cannot remove ours;
* anything that goes wrong (no python3, the proxy cannot start) is "no network" with a fixed log code, never a refusal
  of the command and never a wider network; a command that is not sandboxed gets no proxy at all;
* settings reach the policy (pre-allowed domains, ports, prompt, record, cleartext), and changed settings rebuild the proxy.

Run: cd m2c && pytest tests/tools/test_tools_shell_network.py -v

m2c/tests/tools/test_tools_shell_sandbox.py -- Patch C11a: how the shell tool uses the sandbox (``ShellConfig.sandbox``).

No bubblewrap is needed.  ``m2c.sandbox.get_status`` is replaced by a fake that says "available" or "not available,
for this reason", and ``m2c.sandbox.wrap_command`` by one that builds a command through ``fake-bwrap``: a small
script that records that it was used and then runs what follows the ``--``.  So these tests prove the WIRING:

* auto: sandbox when the probe passed; otherwise unsandboxed, with the warning, once, and the reason;
* required: no sandbox, no command, a fixed error;
* off: the probe is never even asked, the warning says why;
* once the probe passed, a profile that cannot be built NOW is a refusal and never a quiet run without the sandbox
  (otherwise ``ln -s x .git`` would be a way for the model to switch the sandbox off);
* nothing may call the sandbox active, or say "full privileges", when it is the other way round;
* the status lines: 129..159 under the sandbox read "(terminated by signal N)".

The real bubblewrap is exercised in tests/sandbox/test_sandbox_escape.py and test_sandbox_shell_regression.py.

Run: cd m2c && pytest tests/tools/test_tools_shell_sandbox.py -v
